<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Waldeck</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1918 CE (approx.) · capital Arolsen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small north Hessian state of the counts and princes of Waldeck, known for hiring out soldiers and for surviving as a German state until 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Schwalenberg acquired Waldeck castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eder-river">Eder</a> in the 12th century and took its name, emerging as counts of Waldeck by about 1180. They built up a county in the uplands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, often under Hessian pressure, and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 16th century. In 1625 they inherited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-pyrmont">County of Pyrmont</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts, raised to princes in 1712, ruled a poor, rural land of small towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-arolsen">Arolsen</a>, where they built a baroque palace. Revenue came partly from hiring out regiments to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, including troops used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frederick-of-waldeck">George Frederick of Waldeck</a> served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> field marshal under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Waldeck joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-german-confederation">North German Confederation</a> and in 1868 handed its administration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> while keeping its prince. The last prince abdicated in 1918; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-waldeck">Free State of Waldeck</a> lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-pyrmont">Pyrmont</a> in 1922 and merged into Prussia in 1929. The area is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-waldeck.jpg" alt="Principality of Waldeck" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: derivative work: Orange Tuesday (talk) Flag_of_Germany_(2-3).svg: Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Waldeck_before_1830.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1918 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arolsen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Frederick of Waldeck, Friedrich Anton Ulrich, Friedrich of Waldeck-Pyrmont</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Waldeck-Pyrmont, County of Waldeck</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1549&amp;focus=principality-of-waldeck" aria-label="Principality of Waldeck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4884 401 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Waldeck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5204 657L5229 694M5204 657L5199 691M5204 657L5300 622M5204 657L5204 655M5204 657L5209 637"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5204 655h0M5209 637h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1549&amp;focus=principality-of-waldeck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
